{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-351160",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int 2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{t}+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $r(0)=⟨0,0⟩.$ Substituting $t=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡0+{C}_{1},\\,2{e}^{0}+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨{C}_{1},\\,2+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=0$ and ${C}_{2}=-2.$ Therefore, the position vector at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{e}^{t}-2⟩.\n$$\n\nTo find the position vector at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we substitute $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the above, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2{e}^{\\pi /2}-2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
